WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.454
After carefully examining what Ernest had found, I determined that they were cassava plants.

2
00:00:05.455 --> 00:00:11.083
With the plants' roots, we could lose every other food that we possessed and still not go hungry.

3
00:00:11.084 --> 00:00:15.373
"If we can collect enough roots," I said, "we'll attempt to make bread.

4
00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:17.573
But the plant can be poisonous.

5
00:00:17.574 --> 00:00:21.931
We'll need to take great care in preparing the flour and baking the bread.

6
00:00:21.932 --> 00:00:25.321
First we must finish unloading the tub boat and the raft."

7
00:00:25.322 --> 00:00:30.424
That job took us the rest of the afternoon and part of the next morning.

8
00:00:30.425 --> 00:00:33.965
I had intended to spend the rest of the day at Falconhurst.

9
00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:38.262
But the sea was calm, so I decided to make another trip to the wreck.

10
00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:43.230
Ernest and Franz returned to the tree house with my wife while Jack lingered on the beach.

11
00:00:43.231 --> 00:00:47.874
"You want to go with us, don't you?" I said to him. "Well, come on then."

12
00:00:47.875 --> 00:00:57.030
Jack grinned and quickly followed Fritz and me onto the tub boat.

13
00:00:57.031 --> 00:01:00.582
When we reached the wreck, Jack went down into the hold.

14
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:04.616
I soon heard a loud rattling noise as he joined me on deck.

15
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:08.107
"Look what I found—a wheelbarrow!" he said.

16
00:01:08.108 --> 00:01:11.285
"This is just the thing for bringing home potatoes."

17
00:01:11.286 --> 00:01:15.884
He was then followed by Fritz, who had made an even more important discovery.

18
00:01:15.885 --> 00:01:20.422
"I found a small sailboat!" he announced. "All the parts are packed together.

19
00:01:20.423 --> 00:01:23.371
There are even a couple of small brass cannons!"

20
00:01:23.372 --> 00:01:26.764
If he was right, then this would be a most useful item.

21
00:01:26.765 --> 00:01:30.131
I hurried to look at the sailboat.

22
00:01:30.132 --> 00:01:33.430
"Oh," I said with disappointment in my voice.

23
00:01:33.431 --> 00:01:36.192
Yes, it was a sailboat.

24
00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:40.150
But it was going to take a lot of work to put it together and make it seaworthy.

25
00:01:40.151 --> 00:01:44.885
At the moment we only had time enough to get something to eat and hurry to the tub boat.

26
00:01:44.886 --> 00:01:53.440
We carried home a copper boiler, iron plates, tobacco graters, and three wheelbarrows.

27
00:01:53.441 --> 00:01:59.540
At Falconhurst my wife and the other boys had gathered a large supply of potatoes and cassava roots.

28
00:01:59.541 --> 00:02:01.558
I praised them lavishly.

29
00:02:01.559 --> 00:02:04.755
"Oh, Father!" blurted out little Franz.

30
00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:09.882
"What will you say when Mother and I give you melons and pumpkins and cucumbers?"

31
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:15.091
"You little chatterbox," my wife said fondly. "You've let out my secret!

32
00:02:15.092 --> 00:02:18.786
I wanted to surprise Father when my plants sprouted."

33
00:02:18.787 --> 00:02:21.915
I was puzzled. "Where did you get the seeds?"

34
00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:24.433
"From my bag, of course," she replied.

35
00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:29.215
"Every time I've gone for potatoes, I've sown seeds in the ground that we've dug up.

36
00:02:29.216 --> 00:02:31.328
I've also planted potatoes."

37
00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:33.468
My wife looked very pleased.

38
00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:37.463
But then she caught sight of the tobacco graters that we'd brought from the wreck.

39
00:02:37.464 --> 00:02:41.354
"You aren't going to make snuff?" she said in alarm.

40
00:02:41.355 --> 00:02:46.937
"No," I said. "Please treat my graters with respect because they'll provide you with fresh bread."

41
00:02:46.938 --> 00:02:52.492
"What connection can there be between tobacco graters and bread?" asked my wife.

42
00:02:52.493 --> 00:02:56.567
"Besides, you need an oven to bake bread, and we don't have one."

43
00:02:56.568 --> 00:02:59.589
"You can't expect real loaves," I said.

44
00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:03.060
"But I think I can bake flat cakes on these iron plates.

45
00:03:03.061 --> 00:03:05.219
We'll have to make flour first."

46
00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:11.735
My wife looked skeptical. "It's probably a good thing we dug potatoes today."

47
00:03:11.736 --> 00:03:17.172
After thoroughly washing the cassava roots, I spread a large sailcloth on the ground.

48
00:03:17.173 --> 00:03:21.021
Then I called the boys and gave them each a tobacco grater.

49
00:03:21.022 --> 00:03:23.872
Everyone worked at grating down the roots.

50
00:03:23.873 --> 00:03:29.564
Luckily, no one was tempted to stop and taste the flour because it looked like wet sawdust.

51
00:03:29.565 --> 00:03:33.836
When we were finished, we put the damp flour in a canvas bag.

52
00:03:33.837 --> 00:03:37.349
I squeezed and squeezed, but the flour was still wet.

53
00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:41.255
We laid the canvas bag on a board and put another board over it.

54
00:03:41.256 --> 00:03:48.232
Then we found the heaviest things we owned—an anvil, iron bars—and set them on the top board.

55
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:52.934
The pressure on the bag was enormous, and the liquid flowed from it to the ground.

56
00:03:52.935 --> 00:03:55.969
"Could we bake some bread now?" asked Fritz.

57
00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.433
"There doesn't seem to be any moisture left."

58
00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:02.641
"Certainly, but I will make only a small sample today.

59
00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:06.406
We must first see how it agrees with Knips and the hens."

60
00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:09.052
I placed an iron plate over the fire.

61
00:04:09.053 --> 00:04:13.095
While it was getting hot, I mixed the flour with water and salt.

62
00:04:13.096 --> 00:04:17.610
After kneading the dough, I laid it on the hot plate to bake.

63
00:04:17.611 --> 00:04:19.668
The cake smelled delicious!

64
00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:22.888
I gave a small amount to the monkey and two hens.

65
00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:29.444
We watched them all evening for any signs of poisoning.

66
00:04:29.445 --> 00:04:33.299
The next morning the animals were fine, so we baked more bread.

67
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.399
Each boy busily prepared his own cake.

68
00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.681
Some of the bread was burned; some was underdone.

69
00:04:39.682 --> 00:04:41.937
But much of it was golden brown.

70
00:04:41.938 --> 00:04:48.134
We were soon enjoying a delicious breakfast of bread and milk.

